using Wolverine;
using StockFeed.Contracts;
using StockFeed.Landing;

namespace StockFeed.Slices.StockFeed;

/// <summary>
/// The Automated Trigger of translate-stock-notice: <c>Event(s) -> View -> TRIGGER -> Command -> Event(s)</c>.
///
/// IT IS A MESSAGE HANDLER FOR THE FOREIGN NOTICE, and that is what a translation's trigger *is* — the
/// realisation of the drawing rather than a departure from it:
///
///   the foreign event   the notice, in the warehouse's vocabulary, never persisted by us
///   the View            the transport's DURABLE INBOX. Every notice that has arrived and not yet succeeded
///                       is a row in it, with retries and dead-lettering we did not write. That is a todo
///                       list; it is simply not a document of ours
///   the trigger         this method: it reads one item of pending work and issues a Command
///   the command         ApplyStockNotice, in OUR vocabulary
///
/// The automation folder's rule is what licenses this — "it does not require the View to be a materialised
/// projection… a durable inbox is a list of pending work. The green box on the diagram is the concept." This is
/// the sharpest case of it in the kit, because here a materialised View is not merely unnecessary, it is
/// impossible: no Marten projection can fold an event that is never in our store.
///
/// THERE IS NO SEPARATE WAKEUP, and that is the correction this file exists to record. The first version of
/// this folder appended the notice as an event of ours and then woke a trigger off it with a Marten
/// subscription — two mechanisms, one of which only existed to undo the other. For a 1:1 translation **the
/// arrival is the wakeup.** The automation pattern's four mechanisms all wake a trigger off events already in
/// our store, and a translation's trigger event is never in our store.
///
/// IT NEVER APPENDS. No IDocumentSession here at all, which makes that a compile error rather than a
/// convention — and this file is where the temptation is strongest, because the notice arrives carrying
/// everything the event would need. Appending it directly is <c>Event -> Processor -> Event</c>, and it would
/// also put the decision outside the only place the rules live.
///
/// WHERE THE RENAME HAPPENS. <c>quantity</c> is the warehouse's word, <c>onHand</c> is ours, and the model says
/// so with mappings="onHand=quantity" on the command. The single line below is the entire anti-corruption
/// layer, and everything downstream of it speaks only our vocabulary.
/// </summary>
public static class StockTranslator
{
    public static async Task Handle(
        StockNoticed notice,
        IMessageBus bus,
        ILogger logger,
        CancellationToken cancellation)
    {
        // InvokeAsync, not PublishAsync: the trigger wants the outcome so a refusal is visible rather than
        // silently dropped. The decider holds the rules; this only reports.
        var outcome = await bus.InvokeAsync<ApplyOutcome>(
            new ApplyStockNotice(notice.ProductId, notice.NoticeId, notice.Quantity, notice.Sequence),
            cancellation);

        // ALWAYS log, including a refusal. A translation whose refusals are invisible is one nobody can trust,
        // and a refused notice is a fact about the BOUNDARY — a feed that starts redelivering or reordering is
        // a feed that has changed, and nobody would otherwise know.
        //
        // Measured, and the reason this is a warning rather than information: in the first version of this
        // folder the refusal happened inside a materialised todo View's status comparison, so a stale notice
        // was refused with no log line anywhere. Running the app was the only thing that found it.
        if (outcome.WasApplied)
            logger.LogInformation(
                "translated notice {NoticeId} for product {ProductId}: onHand {OnHand}, sequence {Sequence}.",
                notice.NoticeId, notice.ProductId, outcome.OnHand, notice.Sequence);
        else
            logger.LogWarning(
                "REFUSED notice {NoticeId} for product {ProductId}: {Rule}. {Detail}",
                notice.NoticeId, notice.ProductId, outcome.Rule, outcome.Detail);

        // AND IT RETURNS Task, NOT THE OUTCOME. Wolverine treats a handler's return value as a cascading
        // message with no opt-out, so returning ApplyOutcome here would try to route it and take the whole
        // outgoing batch down with it. The value is logged, not returned.
    }
}
